Here’s a quick way you can safely bump `packageInfo.version` to `"1.2.3"` without overwriting an identical value:

```js
// If you’re working inside Node.js / a browser environment where
// `packageInfo` is already defined, run this snippet:

if (
    !Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(packageInfo, 'version')
    || packageInfo.version !== '1.2.3'
) {
    packageInfo.version = '1.2.3';
}
```

### What this does

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **Check existence** (`hasOwnProperty`) | Ensures we don’t accidentally read inherited properties. |
| **Compare old vs new** (`!== '1.2.3'`) | Only writes when the value actually changes. |
| **Assign** | Updates the field once both conditions above are satisfied. |

---

#### Quick test

If you’d like to see it work right away, try adding a dummy object:

```js
const packageInfo = {};          // start empty
console.log(JSON.stringify(packageInfo));   // {}

if (
    !Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(packageInfo, 'version')
    || packageInfo.version !== '1.2.3'
) {
    packageInfo.version = '1.2.3';
}
console.log(JSON.stringify(packageInfo));   // {"version":"1.2.3"}
```

Running the same block again will leave the object unchanged because the value matches.

Feel free to drop this into your script wherever you manage `packageInfo`.
